  <text>
    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="6.1">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:IIIEm">III Em</ref> 6.1</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="6.1" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="191" ed="#liebermann"/>Et si uestigium illud de terra illa<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="10" target="#IIIEm_6-1.lnote.oe.10"/> non possit educi, queratur ubicunque suspectum<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="a" target="#IIIEm_6-1.lnote.oe.a"/> fuerit ac dubium.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="la" source="#robertson" n="6.1" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="14" ed="#robertson"/>Et si vestigium illud de terra illa<ptr type="editorNote" n="10" target="#IIIEm_6-1.robnote.fn.10"/> non possit educi, quaeratur ubicunque suspectum fuerit ac dubium.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-q"><ptr type="editorNote" n="1" target="#IIIEm_6-1.robnote.n.1"/><pb n="15" ed="#robertson"/>And if the track cannot be followed beyond the bounds of that estate, search shall be made wherever suspicion or doubt attaches.</ab>
        <note type="comparison" source="#liebermann" n="191"><ref target="clause:VAs_2">V As 2</ref></note>
        <note type="comparisonPrinted" source="#liebermann">Vgl. V As. 2</note>
        <note type="editorial">Latin only; no German translation in Liebermann. Liebermann compares V As 2.</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IIIEm_6-1.lnote.oe.10" n="10" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">191</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ipsa <hi rend="italic">T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ipsa <hi rend="italic">T</hi></seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IIIEm_6-1.lnote.oe.a" n="a" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">130</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">And gif man þæt trod</hi> (oder <hi rend="italic">spor?</hi>) <hi rend="italic">ne mæge utlædan of þam lande</hi> las Or. laut V As 2. Hu 5. Duns 1. Die Spurfolge dringt in die einzelnen Bauernställe desjenigen Landgutes ein, das den Spurfaden nicht hinauszuleiten vermochte, wo sich Vieh von nicht bekannter Herkunft findet.—<hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Spurfolge 4a</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">And gif man þæt trod</hi> (or <hi rend="italic">spor?</hi>) <hi rend="italic">ne mæge utlædan of þam lande</hi> was the reading of the original, according to V As 2. Hu 5. Duns 1. The pursuit of the trail enters the individual peasants’ cattle-sheds on the estate that was unable to lead the trail out of it, wherever cattle of unknown origin are found.—<hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Spurfolge 4a</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IIIEm_6-1.robnote.n.1" n="1" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">299</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Cf. V As. 2.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IIIEm_6-1.robnote.fn.10" n="10" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">14</biblScope></bibl><label>illa</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">ipsa</hi> T.</seg></note>
      </div>
    </body>
